 "article": {
  "title": "Return to the Moon: A New Space Race",
  "topic": "科技·航天探索",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 315,
  "paras": [
   "For the first time in more than fifty years, human beings are preparing to walk on the Moon again. In the 1960s, the race to the Moon was a contest between two superpowers, driven by politics as much as by science. Today the players are different: not only national space agencies but also private companies, and the goal is no longer to plant a flag and leave, but to stay. This time, however, the reasons for going are more practical than symbolic.",
   "The plan is ambitious. Engineers talk of building a permanent base near the lunar south pole, where deep craters hold water ice in permanent shadow. That ice could be melted for drinking water, split into oxygen to breathe, and turned into fuel for rockets. In other words, the Moon could become a kind of filling station on the long road to Mars.",
   "Not everyone is convinced. Critics point out that the same money could fund telescopes, medical research or efforts to fight climate change closer to home. They also warn that sending people into space is far more expensive and risky than sending robots. Supporters reply that human explorers can do things machines cannot, and that the technology developed along the way often ends up improving daily life on Earth. The debate, they say, is not about whether to explore, but about how to do it wisely.",
   "Whatever the outcome, the new lunar effort is already changing how we think about exploration. It is no longer a single dramatic sprint, but a long, patient project shared by many countries and many companies. As one engineer put it, the Moon is not the destination; it is where the journey truly begins."
  ],
  "zh": [
   "五十多年来，人类再次准备踏上月球。20 世纪 60 年代的登月之争是超级大国之间的较量，政治与科学同样在推动它；而今天的参与方已大不相同——不只是各国航天机构，还有私营企业，目标也不再是插旗就走，而是留下来。",
   "计划颇为宏大。工程师们谈到要在月球南极附近建一座永久基地，那里的深坑在永久阴影中封存着水冰。这些冰可以融化成饮用水、分解出氧气供呼吸，还能制成火箭燃料——换句话说，月球可能成为通往火星路上的“加油站”。",
   "但并非所有人都信服。批评者指出，同样的钱本可以用于望远镜、医学研究，或在离地球更近的地方应对气候变化；他们还提醒，送人上太空远比送机器人昂贵和危险。支持者则回应，人类探索者能做到机器做不到的事，而且沿途研发的技术常常最终改善了地球上的日常生活。",
   "无论结果如何，这场新的登月努力已经在改变我们对探索的理解：它不再是一次激动人心的短跑，而是一项由许多国家和企业共同参与、长期耐心的工程。正如一位工程师所说，月球不是终点，真正的旅程从这里开始。"
